- F-146 **The compiled checker accepts arithmetic in a size, and compares two sizes by their written form once it has computed each operation on numerals.** It builds P-55, the curator's decision on arithmetic in a size. Two sizes are the same where their written forms match once each operation on numerals is computed. They differ only where they are two different numerals or two different size parameters, so the checker calls a `typecase` clause unreachable that walk reaches at equal arguments (row 683, contested). Any other pair, such as a size name against a numeral, or two operations written differently, is not known to differ. Neither path infers a static parameter that a parameter type writes inside an operation (row 767). The compiled class loader can make a size outside its kind's range, as a `nat` above 4294967295, where walk refuses it (row 675). Gated by `compiler_tests/NatArith*`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md` section 1, "Item 15, 15a with its part (b)", and section 12.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-146"); split: the opening of where-clause sizes, a default under review, is now F-?1; the title's second half from P-55 and the report's section 1 (nEq); rows 683, 767 and 675 given their subjects from their ledger cells; row 682 and row 307 moved with the opening; fix round: the comparison's three answers said as pEqv gives them (the same, different, not known to differ); "beyond its kind" said as a nat above 4294967295 (row 675)
- F-?1 **The compiled checker opens a value of a trait whose `comprises` clause lists a type at a `where`-clause size: it gives each listed type's `nat` and `int` `where`-clause sizes a fresh name for the call.** In `trait NatParam comprises { N[\n\] } where [\ nat n \]`, a `NatParam` passed where `N[\$n\]` is expected, `$n` being the checker's inference variable for the called function's size parameter, binds `$n` to the fresh size, and the call's result type carries it. An opened size is not known to differ from any size. Walk's `comprises` check at load reads such a size as any size, the same at each of its places; at a call, walk binds the argument's own size. This is a default, way 4a of fork 3 (a size known only at run time) in PLAN's section "Pavol's answers, in the order they are needed", item 13. An agent landed it, and it waits for the curator's review (review-queue item 30); P-85, his position that the array questions are open, records it as the one exception. The code generator does not compile such a trait: "Can't compile TraitDecl NatParamT" (row 307; `compiler_tests/XXXNatOpenRun`). In row 682's program, with `trait NatParamT comprises { Nt[\n\] } where [\nat n\]`, the checker refuses `f(x: NatParamT)` beside `f[\nat n\](x: Nt[\n\])` as two declarations of one parameter type, where walk runs the pair (row 682, contested). F-251 holds the same opening for a type variable. Gated by `compiler_tests/NatOpenChecker`, and under walk by `tests/NatOpenWalk` and `NatOpenUnlistedExtender`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md` section 1, "Fork 3, way 4a, under Q13.3", and section 12.
  from: explorations/compile-ladder/rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-146 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-?1"); "array fork 3 at its default" said as P-85 and review-queue item 30 say it; the NatParam example and the binding of $n from section 1; walk's gates NatOpenWalk and NatOpenUnlistedExtender added (the assessment); fix round: walk's reading at load set apart from its binding at a call (section 1, the walk point); PLAN's section named; what P-85 contributes said; the failing stage named, the code generator (compiler_tests/XXXNatOpenRun.test; CodeGen.java:1557); $n and row 682's names said
- F-228 **Under walk, a reduction or a comprehension that writes no static argument instantiates its big operator by a declared type, else by the join of its elements' run-time types, else at its bound, where an F-bounded parameter stays open.** It builds P-48. The declared type is a local variable's, or a top-level or local function's declared return type, for either also where the clause form ends a block. A method's return type, a top-level variable, a field, an assignment, a function expression, a branch, a call's argument and another clause form's body do not direct it. Otherwise the clauses run once into the library's collector, `__elemsOf`, which keeps the elements in order, typed at the join of their run-time types, and the operator is instantiated at that join; the reduction then runs over the collected elements, so each body runs once. A declared type or a join that does not fit falls back to the instance that no hint gives: a plain-bounded parameter takes its bound, and an F-bounded one stays open (F-143). So a `SUM` of `ZZ32` and `RR64` values, whose join `Number` is outside the bound, runs with each `+` at its operands' types. The same holds with no element and no declared type. With the big operator so instantiated, the library types `__bigOperator`'s local function `body(i: I)` at the element type `I`. A big operator whose declared result is a trait of its own is read through the `BigReduction`, `Comprehension` or `BigOperator` that the trait extends (row 718, fixed). Still wrong, each a ledger row: an empty clause form that no declared type directs takes its bound or stays open (row 716); a comprehension that no declared type reaches is built at its elements' run-time class, as `ArrayList[\Int\]`, which a declared `List[\ZZ32\]` refuses (rows 20, 717); a clause form that is a call's argument gets no declared type (rows 510, 705). Gated by `tests/UnwrittenListMapComprehensionWalk`, `UnwrittenComprehension*Walk`, `ComprehensionTypedBindingWalk`, `UnwrittenSetBigOperatorsWalk`, `UnwrittenEmptyFloatSumWalk`, `IntMapComprehensionTypedWalk` and `UserBigOperatorResultTraitWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md` sections 1, 5 (5.3 lists the contexts) and 10, decision 6; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-overload-if/REPORT.md` section 1, "Row 718".
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-228"); the third step given both cases, a plain bound and F-143's open parameter (rung-walk-elements/REPORT.md section 1, item 3); the title's "the declared type of its value" narrowed to the contexts reached, with those not reached from section 5.3; moved here: "by the coordinator's call (the walk probe's way 6 + 7), and lifts D2", ways 6 and 7 of perf-probes/walk-plain-bound/REPORT.md and decision D2, a plain-bounded parameter kept at BottomType; the collector's lines Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1318-1437 (now to :1439), EvaluatorBase.instantiateBigOperator and the per-argument caches, EvaluatorBase.libraryBigResult; fix round: the fallback of a declared type or join that does not fit added (section 1; section 10, decision 6; the Number example from the skill's revival-changes.md, "Static parameters"); the typed body tied back to the instantiation, not to row 718's repair (Library/FortressLibrary.fss:1314); "Left open" renamed, so that it does not read as the open type, with what goes wrong in each row; the collector and the block tail of both contexts said
- F-238 **Under walk's load check, an exclusion that two declarations with static parameters of their own show only when each is read at a symbolic instantiation is withdrawn where their instances do not exclude.** A declaration with static parameters of its own is defined in F-252. The check first reads two generic functions of one name and one list of static parameters at one shared symbolic instantiation, and two instances of one generic object as two object types that cannot be extended, so they exclude. So `o[\T\](x: P[\T\])` beside `o[\T\](x: Q[\T\])`, with `trait P[\T\] excludes { Q[\T\] }`, excludes at one `T`, though a type that extends `P[\ZZ32\]` and `Q[\String\]` has both. Walk then reads such an exclusion again over the instances of each. Instances of one generic type exclude only where their static arguments cannot be equal. Any other exclusion stands where the two still exclude with their static parameters renamed apart. Or, read at the shared instantiation, it stands where every generic type that both extend forces their static arguments to be equal, since no type extends two instances of one generic trait: so `Array` and `ImmutableArray` exclude at every pair of instances through `ReadableArray`. A pair whose exclusion is withdrawn goes on to the reading on declared domains (F-252), and a refusal there names the rule that the pair breaks (F-257). Gated, as refusals at load, by `tests/GenericBoundsNoMeetWalk`, `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk`, `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` and `GenericOverloadInstancesApartWalk`; as programs that load, by `GenericBoundsMeetWalk`, `GenericFBoundsMeetWalk`, `GenericPairExcludingWalk`, `GenericSizePairNestedWalk`, `ShuffleTest` and `GenericPairExcludesTogetherWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-overload-if/REPORT.md` section 1, "Row 373", and section 9.
  by: the clarity pass; note: reorganized (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f13/proposals.md, "F-238"); kept to its own claim, the withdrawal and its ReadableArray exception, in the present tense, since the shared reading still runs first (FGenericFunction.java:43-64, getSymbolic); the o pair from rung-walk-overload-if/REPORT.md section 1; what F-252, F-257 and F-258 own left to them: the three rules on declared domains, the Return Type Rule at the holding instance, the message, the whole-type reading and row 787; moved here: FGenericFunction.java:43-64, the F-bounds read from the declarations and a symbolic size unified with a size parameter as types/SymbolicNat.unifyStaticArg reads it (the report's section 1, meetParams); fix round: the title widened to both old readings, the generic object's instances too (the report's section 1; decision 1); the alternative that an exclusion read apart stands restored, where the first round's "only" denied it; "first" marks the old reading; the gates grouped as refusals and loads (the report's section 10)
- F-239 **Under walk, an `if` clause without a binding takes a `Boolean` by its value, an object named `Just` of a trait named `Maybe` as true and one named `Nothing` as false, and refuses every other value, "If clause did not return boolean".** Walk tests the names only, so a `Just` is true whatever it holds, and a program's own `Just` below its own trait `Maybe` counts too. The refused values are a number, a string, `()`, a tuple, a function, and an object that is neither a `Just` nor a `Nothing`. A refusal while a top-level variable is initialised counts as a refusal at load. The specification's `if` chapter allows a `Boolean` only (`Specification/basic/expressions/if.tex:54`; row 746). This reading is a default, the coordinator's call at climb batch 16, which waits for the curator's review (PLAN, "Climb batch 16, listed for his review"). Gated by `tests/IfClauseObjectWalk`, `IfClauseNumberWalk`, `IfClauseStringWalk`, `maybeTest` and `naturalsTest`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-overload-if/REPORT.md` section 1, "Row 673".
